ligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354410 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85284" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354410 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are 